		<CGIFreeText type="e">
			<_id type="a">GUID 7e32e0b5-0891-4c79-a19d-33f6075d5610</_id>
			<m_type type="a">46</m_type>
			<m_pModelObject type="r">
				<IHandle type="e">
				</IHandle>
			</m_pModelObject>
			<m_pParent type="s">GUID 1cceb05e-5bff-48d0-a091-c0a91d11a2dd</m_pParent>
			<m_name type="p">
				<CGIText type="e">
					<m_str type="a">This is a variant of the original HomeAlarm sample, redesigned using ports.%0D
%0D
Ports are a new UML2.0 modeling concept that allows strong encapsulation of classes from the%0D
environment. In this case, the Hardware and Alarm Controller are decoupled by specifying ports%0D
as their boundary interaction points.%0D
%0D
This sample also demonstrates the usage of the new Rhapsody 5.0 Requirement modeling concept - the AlarmPkg %0D
contains a set of requirements defined for the Home Alarm system. These requirements are realized by the use cases %0D
using &lt;&lt;trace&gt;&gt; dependencies as shown in the Requirements Vs. Use Cases diagram.%0D
%0D
It is recommended to refer to the original sample in order to compare the traditional class-based approach vs%0D
the port-based approach.%0D
%0D
</m_str>
					<m_style type="a">Arial; 10 0 0 0 1 </m_style>
					<m_color type="p">
						<IColor type="e">
							<m_fgColor type="a">32</m_fgColor>
							<m_bgColor type="a">32</m_bgColor>
							<m_bgFlag type="a">0</m_bgFlag>
						</IColor>
					</m_color>
					<m_position type="a">1 3 13.7315789473684  </m_position>
					<m_nIdent type="a">0</m_nIdent>
					<m_bImplicitSetRectPoints type="a">0</m_bImplicitSetRectPoints>
					<m_nOrientationCtrlPt type="a">8</m_nOrientationCtrlPt>
				</CGIText>
			</m_name>
			<m_drawBehavior type="a">4096</m_drawBehavior>
			<m_bIsPreferencesInitialized type="a">1</m_bIsPreferencesInitialized>
			<m_points type="a">4 52 77  827 77  827 320  52 320  </m_points>
			<m_text type="a">This is a variant of the original HomeAlarm sample, redesigned using ports.%0D
%0D
Ports are a new UML2.0 modeling concept that allows strong encapsulation of classes from the%0D
environment. In this case, the Hardware and Alarm Controller are decoupled by specifying ports%0D
as their boundary interaction points.%0D
%0D
This sample also demonstrates the usage of the new Rhapsody 5.0 Requirement modeling concept - the AlarmPkg %0D
contains a set of requirements defined for the Home Alarm system. These requirements are realized by the use cases %0D
using &lt;&lt;trace&gt;&gt; dependencies as shown in the Requirements Vs. Use Cases diagram.%0D
%0D
It is recommended to refer to the original sample in order to compare the traditional class-based approach vs%0D
the port-based approach.%0D
%0D
</m_text>
